      ":question/problem": "To which of the following sets do all the rational numbers belong?\n\nI. $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$\n\nII. $\\mathbb{Q}$\n\nIII. $\\mathbb{C}$\n\nIV. $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We must check whether each set contains all the rational numbers.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$ contains the nonnegative integers. For instance, $-1$ is in the given class but not in $\\mathbb{Z}_0^{+}$. Therefore, this set does not contain all the rational numbers.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Q}$ contains all rational numbers. By the number-set hierarchy and the sign restrictions, every member of the given class belongs to $\\mathbb{Q}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{C}$ contains all complex numbers. By the number-set hierarchy and the sign restrictions, every member of the given class belongs to $\\mathbb{C}$.\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$ contains the positive real numbers. For instance, $-1$ is in the given class but not in $\\mathbb{R}^{+}$. Therefore, this set does not contain all the rational numbers.\n\nTherefore, select \"II and III only\".",
